#. Translators: {name} is the test's name, {steps} what it does.
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{name}: {steps}\n"
"\n"
"This really deletes these files from this computer: the user accounts and "
"passwords (/etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group, /etc/gshadow), the list of "
"disks (/etc/fstab), the login rules (/etc/pam.d) and the administrator rules "
"(/etc/sudoers). Your own files and apps aren't touched.\n"
"\n"
"Save your work and close your apps first: the computer restarts as soon as "
"they're deleted.\n"
"\n"
"If putting them back doesn't work: when a start hangs or stays broken, hold "
"the power button until the computer turns off, then turn it on. After 2 bad "
"starts, the next start is the earlier version kept for Undo the last update, "
"which has its own copy of these settings."
msgstr ""

#. Translators: {name} is the test's name, {steps} what it does, {save} what to do before it starts.
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{name}: {steps}\n"
"\n"
"When it comes back, a message says the test worked, and the test copy is "
"removed. Don't turn the computer off during the test unless the steps say "
"to. {save}\n"
"\n"
"The test copy takes the place of the earlier version kept for Undo the last "
"update, so Undo has nothing to go back to until the next update."
msgstr ""

